--------------------------------------------------
External knowledge that might be helpful: 
Categories: Geospatial functions


## ST_DWITHIN

Returns TRUE if the minimum geodesic distance between two points (two GEOGRAPHY objects) is within the specified distance. Otherwise, returns FALSE.
If the parameters are GEOGRAPHY values that are not points (e.g. lines or polygons), this returns TRUE or FALSE based on the minimum geodesic distance between the two closest points of the two values.

Tip You can use the search optimization service to improve the performance of queries that call this function.
For details, see Search Optimization Service.

## Syntax

ST_DWITHIN( <geography_expression_1> , <geography_expression_2> , <distance_in_meters> )


## Arguments


geography_expression_1The argument must be an expression of type GEOGRAPHY.

geography_expression_2The argument must be an expression of type GEOGRAPHY.

distance_in_metersThe argument must be an expression of type REAL. The distance is in meters.


## Returns

Returns a BOOLEAN.

## Usage notes


Returns NULL if any input is NULL.


## Examples

This returns TRUE because the distance in meters between two points 1 degree apart along the equator is less than 150,000 meters:

SELECT ST_DWITHIN (ST_MAKEPOINT(0, 0), ST_MAKEPOINT(1, 0), 150000);
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
| ST_DWITHIN (ST_MAKEPOINT(0, 0), ST_MAKEPOINT(1, 0), 150000) |
|-------------------------------------------------------------|
| True                                                        |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
The table structure information is ({database name: {schema name: [table name]}}): 
{'GEO_OPENSTREETMAP': {'GEO_OPENSTREETMAP': ['HISTORY_RELATIONS', 'HISTORY_CHANGESETS', 'PLANET_FEATURES', 'PLANET_FEATURES_POINTS', 'HISTORY_LAYERS', 'PLANET_WAYS', 'HISTORY_NODES', 'PLANET_NODES', 'PLANET_RELATIONS', 'PLANET_LAYERS']}}
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• PLANET_FEATURES."geometry": polygon/line/point geometry of every OSM feature; cast with TO_GEOGRAPHY() for spatial tests (ST_CONTAINS, ST_INTERSECTS, ST_DWITHIN, etc.).  
• PLANET_FEATURES."all_tags": JSON array of key/value objects; flatten to locate the multipolygon that carries `"wikidata" = 'Q1095'` and to inspect other tags.  
• PLANET_FEATURES."osm_way_id", "osm_id", "feature_type": identifiers that can be matched to relation members and filtered by geometry type.  
• PLANET_RELATIONS."geometry": overall geometry of each relation; lets us test if it overlaps / is contained in the same geographic extent as the Q1095 multipolygon.  
• PLANET_RELATIONS."all_tags": JSON array of tags on the relation itself; flatten to require a `"name"` tag, to exclude any `"wikidata"` tag, etc.  
• PLANET_RELATIONS."members": JSON array that lists IDs of nodes / ways / relations included in the relation; flatten to join back to PLANET_FEATURES so we can count how many of its members carry a `"wikidata"` tag.  
• PLANET_RELATIONS."id": relation identifier – final object we need to name.  
• PLANET_FEATURES_POINTS / PLANET_WAYS (optional): allow us to enrich the search for members that are points or ways rather than polygons.  

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows) that move from simple inspection to the final aggregation needed to answer the task.  All names are wrapped in double‑quotes to avoid case/keyword issues.

```sql
-- 1. Peek at a handful of features and their tags
SELECT *
FROM "GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."PLANET_FEATURES"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Locate the multipolygon whose wikidata tag equals 'Q1095'
SELECT pf."osm_way_id",
       pf."osm_id",
       pf."feature_type",
       tag.value:"key"::STRING  AS "tag_key",
       tag.value:"value"::STRING AS "tag_val"
FROM   "GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."PLANET_FEATURES" pf,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => pf."all_tags") tag
WHERE  LOWER(tag.value:"key"::STRING) = 'wikidata'
  AND  tag.value:"value"::STRING = 'Q1095'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Fetch the bounding‑box WKT of the Q1095 multipolygon
SELECT ST_ASWKT(ST_ENVELOPE(TO_GEOGRAPHY(pf."geometry"))) AS "q1095_bbox"
FROM   "GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."PLANET_FEATURES" pf,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => pf."all_tags") tag
WHERE  LOWER(tag.value:"key"::STRING) = 'wikidata'
  AND  tag.value:"value"::STRING = 'Q1095'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. List relations that spatially intersect that bbox
WITH q1095 AS (
  SELECT ST_ENVELOPE(TO_GEOGRAPHY(pf."geometry")) AS geom
  FROM   "GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."PLANET_FEATURES" pf,
         LATERAL FLATTEN(input => pf."all_tags") tag
  WHERE  LOWER(tag.value:"key"::STRING) = 'wikidata'
    AND  tag.value:"value"::STRING = 'Q1095'
  LIMIT 1
)
SELECT pr."id",
       pr."all_tags"
FROM   "GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."PLANET_RELATIONS" pr, q1095
WHERE  ST_INTERSECTS(TO_GEOGRAPHY(pr."geometry"), q1095.geom)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Among those relations, keep only ones WITH a 'name' tag and WITHOUT a 'wikidata' tag
WITH candidate_relations AS (
  SELECT pr."id", pr."all_tags"
  FROM   "GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."PLANET_RELATIONS" pr
)
SELECT cr."id"                                        AS "relation_id",
       MAX(CASE WHEN t.value:"key"::STRING = 'name'     THEN t.value:"value"::STRING END) AS "relation_name",
       BOOLOR(t.value:"key"::STRING = 'wikidata')      AS "has_wikidata"
FROM   candidate_relations cr,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => cr."all_tags") t
GROUP  BY cr."id"
HAVING MAX(CASE WHEN t.value:"key"::STRING = 'name' THEN 1 END) = 1          -- has name
   AND BOOLOR(t.value:"key"::STRING = 'wikidata') = FALSE                    -- no wikidata
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Flatten members of those candidate relations
SELECT pr."id"                    AS "relation_id",
       mem.value:"type"::STRING   AS "member_type",
       mem.value:"ref"::NUMBER    AS "member_ref"
FROM   "GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."PLANET_RELATIONS" pr,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => pr."members") mem
WHERE  pr."id" IN ( /* paste relation_ids from query 5 or wrap in sub‑query */ )
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Check which of those members (ways or relations) carry a wikidata tag
WITH members AS (
  SELECT pr."id"                  AS relation_id,
         mem.value:"type"::STRING AS member_type,
         mem.value:"ref"::NUMBER  AS member_ref
  FROM   "GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."PLANET_RELATIONS" pr,
         LATERAL FLATTEN(input => pr."members") mem
  WHERE  pr."id" IN ( /* candidate relation ids */ )
)
SELECT m."relation_id",
       COUNT(*)                             AS "member_cnt",
       COUNT(CASE WHEN tag.value:"key"::STRING = 'wikidata' THEN 1 END) AS "members_with_wikidata"
FROM   members m
JOIN   "GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."PLANET_FEATURES" pf
       ON  pf."osm_way_id" = m."member_ref"         -- match to ways
     OR pf."osm_id"     = m."member_ref"            -- match to multipolygons stored by relation id
LEFT  JOIN LATERAL FLATTEN(input => pf."all_tags") tag
GROUP BY m."relation_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Retrieve the names of relations plus the count of ALL features they include (irrespective of wikidata)
WITH members AS (
  SELECT pr."id" AS relation_id
       , mem.value:"ref"::NUMBER AS member_ref
  FROM   "GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."PLANET_RELATIONS" pr,
         LATERAL FLATTEN(input => pr."members") mem
  WHERE  pr."id" IN ( /* candidate relations */ )
)
SELECT r."id" AS relation_id,
       MAX(CASE WHEN t.value:"key"::STRING = 'name' THEN t.value:"value"::STRING END) AS relation_name,
       COUNT(*)           AS total_members
FROM   "GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."PLANET_RELATIONS" r
JOIN   members m
       ON m.relation_id = r."id"
JOIN   LATERAL FLATTEN(input => r."all_tags") t
GROUP  BY r."id"
ORDER  BY total_members DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Combine everything: pick the relation with the most members that has at least one member with wikidata
WITH q1095_geom AS (
  SELECT ST_ENVELOPE(TO_GEOGRAPHY(pf."geometry")) AS geom
  FROM   "GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."PLANET_FEATURES" pf,
         LATERAL FLATTEN(input => pf."all_tags") tag
  WHERE  LOWER(tag.value:"key"::STRING) = 'wikidata'
    AND  tag.value:"value"::STRING = 'Q1095'
  LIMIT 1
),
candidate_relations AS (
  SELECT pr."id", pr."all_tags"
  FROM   "GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."PLANET_RELATIONS" pr, q1095_geom
  WHERE  ST_INTERSECTS(TO_GEOGRAPHY(pr."geometry"), q1095_geom.geom)
),
named_no_wd AS (
  SELECT cr."id" AS relation_id
  FROM   candidate_relations cr,
         LATERAL FLATTEN(input => cr."all_tags") tag
  GROUP  BY cr."id"
  HAVING MAX(CASE WHEN tag.value:"key"::STRING = 'name' THEN 1 END) = 1
     AND BOOLOR(tag.value:"key"::STRING = 'wikidata') = FALSE
),
members AS (
  SELECT r.relation_id,
         mem.value:"ref"::NUMBER AS member_ref
  FROM   named_no_wd r
  JOIN   "GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."PLANET_RELATIONS" pr
         ON pr."id" = r.relation_id,
         LATERAL FLATTEN(input => pr."members") mem
),
members_with_wd AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT m.relation_id
  FROM   members m
  JOIN   "GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."PLANET_FEATURES" pf
         ON pf."osm_way_id" = m.member_ref
       OR pf."osm_id"     = m.member_ref,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => pf."all_tags") tag
  WHERE  LOWER(tag.value:"key"::STRING) = 'wikidata'
)
SELECT r."id"                                                        AS relation_id,
       MAX(CASE WHEN t.value:"key"::STRING = 'name' THEN t.value:"value"::STRING END) AS relation_name,
       COUNT(*)                                                      AS total_members
FROM   named_no_wd r
JOIN   "GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."GEO_OPENSTREETMAP"."PLANET_RELATIONS" pr
       ON pr."id" = r.relation_id
JOIN   LATERAL FLATTEN(input => pr."all_tags") t
JOIN   members m
       ON m.relation_id = r.relation_id
JOIN   members_with_wd w
       ON w.relation_id = r.relation_id      -- keep only those with ≥1 wikidata member
GROUP  BY r."id"
ORDER  BY total_members DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Return just the 'name' of the single top‑scoring relation
WITH ranked_relations AS (
  /* identical CTE stack as query 9, but add ROW_NUMBER */
  /* ... (omitted for brevity – paste query 9’s body) ... */
)
SELECT relation_name
FROM   ranked_relations
ORDER  BY total_members DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

These progressively reveal the geometry of the Wikidata Q1095 multipolygon, identify relations in the same area, filter for “has name – no wikidata – at least one member with wikidata”, count how many features each relation contains, and finally select the one with the greatest count (the answer the business problem asks for).
